"""Static copy: background texts, general FAQ and glossary. Kept separate from build.py."""

BACKGROUND = {
    "christmas": "Christmas Day marks the Christian celebration of the birth of Jesus and falls on 25 December in most Western churches. Many Orthodox churches that follow the Julian calendar celebrate it on 7 January instead. It is a public holiday in most countries with a Christian heritage, and in many of them shops, offices and public transport close or run reduced timetables.",
    "christmas-eve": "Christmas Eve is the day before Christmas Day, 24 December. In several countries, including Germany, Poland and Estonia, the main family meal and the exchange of gifts take place on the evening of the 24th. It is a public holiday in some countries, and a shortened working day in some workplaces elsewhere.",
    "new-years-eve": "New Year's Eve is the last day of the Gregorian calendar year, 31 December. The new year arrives first on islands such as Kiritimati (UTC+14) and last in time zones such as UTC−12, so the celebrations with fireworks and midnight countdowns roll around the world over about 26 hours.",
    "new-years-day": "New Year's Day, 1 January, is the first day of the Gregorian calendar and one of the few public holidays observed in almost every country. Some countries and communities also celebrate other new years on different dates, for example Lunar New Year, Nowruz or Rosh Hashanah.",
    "valentines-day": "Valentine's Day on 14 February is a day for cards, flowers and romantic gestures. It takes its name from the Christian feast of Saint Valentine, and its romantic meaning grew through medieval literature. It is not a public holiday in most countries.",
    "st-patricks-day": "St. Patrick's Day on 17 March honours Saint Patrick, the patron saint of Ireland. It is a public holiday in Ireland and Northern Ireland, and is celebrated with parades and green decorations in many places with large Irish communities, such as the United States.",
    "easter": "Easter Sunday celebrates the resurrection of Jesus in the Christian tradition. Western churches calculate it as the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after 21 March, so it can fall between 22 March and 25 April. Orthodox churches use the Julian calendar for the calculation, which means their Easter often falls on a different date.",
    "good-friday": "Good Friday commemorates the crucifixion of Jesus and falls two days before Easter Sunday. Because Easter moves from year to year, Good Friday moves with it. It is a public holiday in many countries across Europe, the Americas and Oceania.",
    "easter-monday": "Easter Monday is the day after Easter Sunday. Like the rest of the Easter holidays it moves each year, and it is a public holiday in many European countries and several others, which often makes Easter a four-day weekend.",
    "april-fools-day": "April Fools' Day on 1 April is a day of practical jokes and hoaxes in many countries. Newspapers, brands and websites often publish made-up stories. It is not a public holiday anywhere that we list.",
    "earth-day": "Earth Day on 22 April has been held since 1970 to raise support for environmental protection. It is now coordinated worldwide, and the United Nations also recognises 22 April as International Mother Earth Day. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday.",
    "mothers-day-us": "In the United States, Canada, Australia and many other countries, Mother's Day falls on the second Sunday of May. The date varies elsewhere: the United Kingdom celebrates Mothering Sunday in March, on the fourth Sunday of Lent, and some countries use a fixed calendar date.",
    "fathers-day-us": "In the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada and many other countries, Father's Day falls on the third Sunday of June. Other countries choose different dates. Germany, for example, observes Father's Day on Ascension Day, and Estonia celebrates it on the second Sunday of November.",
    "memorial-day": "Memorial Day, the last Monday of May, honours members of the United States armed forces who died while serving. It is a federal holiday, and for many Americans it marks the unofficial start of summer.",
    "independence-day-us": "Independence Day, often called the Fourth of July, commemorates the adoption of the Declaration of Independence on 4 July 1776. It is a federal holiday in the United States, usually celebrated with fireworks, parades and barbecues.",
    "labor-day-us": "Labor Day in the United States and Canada falls on the first Monday of September and honours workers. Most other countries mark International Workers' Day on 1 May instead.",
    "halloween": "Halloween on 31 October is the evening before All Saints' Day. It is often linked to older harvest and autumn festivals in the Celtic regions. Costumes, carved pumpkins and trick-or-treating became popular worldwide through the United States. It is not a public holiday in most countries.",
    "international-mens-day": "International Men's Day on 19 November was first marked in Trinidad and Tobago in 1999 and is now observed in many countries. Its themes include the health and wellbeing of men and boys and positive male role models. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday. In Estonia, Father's Day on the second Sunday of November is the better-known day for men.",
    "international-mother-language-day": "International Mother Language Day on 21 February was proclaimed by UNESCO in 1999 and has been observed since 2000. It promotes linguistic and cultural diversity and teaching in children's first language. The date recalls 21 February 1952, when police in Dhaka, in present-day Bangladesh, opened fire on students and others demonstrating for the recognition of Bengali. The day is a public holiday in Bangladesh.",
    "international-womens-day": "International Women's Day on 8 March grew out of the labour and women's suffrage movements in North America and Europe in the early 20th century. The United Nations has marked it since 1975. It is a public holiday in several countries; in many others it is a working day marked with flowers, events and discussions about equality.",
    "pi-day": 'Pi Day on 14 March takes its name from the date written as 3/14 in the American month/day format, the first digits of the number pi (3.14…). The first known Pi Day celebration was organised by physicist Larry Shaw at the Exploratorium science museum in San Francisco in 1988. Since 2020 UNESCO has also marked 14 March as the International Day of Mathematics.',
    "international-day-of-happiness": 'The International Day of Happiness on 20 March was established by the United Nations General Assembly in 2012, following an initiative by Bhutan, and was first held in 2013. It calls for happiness and well-being to be treated as goals of public policy. The World Happiness Report is usually published around this date.',
    "world-water-day": 'World Water Day on 22 March has been held by the United Nations every year since 1993. Each year has a theme about fresh water, such as access to safe drinking water and sanitation. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday.',
    "world-health-day": 'World Health Day on 7 April marks the founding of the World Health Organization, whose constitution came into force on 7 April 1948. WHO has held the day since 1950, each year with a theme from global health. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday.',
    "world-book-day": "World Book and Copyright Day on 23 April was established by UNESCO in 1995 to promote reading, publishing and copyright. The date is linked to the deaths of Miguel de Cervantes and William Shakespeare in 1616 and to the Catalan tradition of giving books and roses on Saint George's Day. The United Kingdom and Ireland hold their own World Book Day on the first Thursday of March.",
    "international-childrens-day": "International Children's Day on 1 June has been observed since 1950, especially in Central and Eastern Europe and in China. Many other countries hold Children's Day on their own dates, and the United Nations marks World Children's Day on 20 November. In a few countries 1 June is a public holiday or a day off school.",
    "world-bicycle-day": 'World Bicycle Day on 3 June was declared by the United Nations General Assembly in 2018. It recognises the bicycle as a simple, affordable and clean means of transport that is also good for health. Cities and cycling clubs in many countries hold group rides and events around the day.',
    "world-environment-day": 'World Environment Day on 5 June is led by the UN Environment Programme and has been held since 1973. Each year a different country hosts the main events around a theme, such as plastic pollution or land restoration. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday.',
    "world-oceans-day": 'World Oceans Day on 8 June was proposed at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro in 1992 and has been observed by the United Nations since 2009. It draws attention to the health of the oceans and to their protection. Aquariums, schools and environmental groups hold events and beach clean-ups around the day.',
    "international-day-of-yoga": "The International Day of Yoga on 21 June was proclaimed by the UN General Assembly in 2014 on India's proposal and was first held in 2015. The date is close to the June solstice, the longest day of the year in the northern hemisphere. Group yoga sessions are held in parks and public squares in many countries.",
    "world-emoji-day": 'World Emoji Day on 17 July was started in 2014 by Jeremy Burge, the founder of the Emojipedia website. The date comes from the calendar emoji, which on Apple devices shows 17 July. Technology companies often announce new emoji around the day.',
    "international-day-of-friendship": 'The International Day of Friendship on 30 July was proclaimed by the United Nations General Assembly in 2011. It promotes friendship between people, countries and cultures. Several countries keep their own Friendship Day on another date; India, for example, marks it on the first Sunday of August.',
    "international-cat-day": 'International Cat Day on 8 August was started in 2002 by the International Fund for Animal Welfare (IFAW). Since 2020 it has been run by the charity International Cat Care. It is a day to celebrate cats and to promote their welfare.',
    "international-dog-day": 'International Dog Day on 26 August began in 2004 as National Dog Day in the United States. It was founded by Colleen Paige, an animal welfare advocate and author. The date is the day her family adopted her first dog from a local animal shelter. The day encourages people to adopt dogs from shelters and rescue groups.',
    "international-day-of-peace": 'The International Day of Peace on 21 September was established by the United Nations in 1981 and has had this fixed date since 2002. The UN calls for a 24-hour ceasefire and non-violence on the day. At UN Headquarters in New York, the Peace Bell is rung to open the observance.',
    "international-coffee-day": 'International Coffee Day on 1 October is promoted by the International Coffee Organization, which launched the official day at Expo 2015 in Milan. It celebrates coffee and draws attention to the farmers who grow it. Some countries keep their own national coffee days on other dates.',
    "world-teachers-day": "World Teachers' Day on 5 October has been held since 1994 and marks the anniversary of the 1966 ILO/UNESCO Recommendation concerning the Status of Teachers. Many countries celebrate their own Teachers' Day on other dates. In Estonia it is celebrated on 5 October, and in many schools older students teach some of the lessons that day.",
    "world-mental-health-day": 'World Mental Health Day on 10 October was first held in 1992 by the World Federation for Mental Health. The World Health Organization supports the day, and each year has a theme. It aims to raise awareness of mental health and to reduce stigma.',
    "world-kindness-day": 'World Kindness Day on 13 November was introduced in 1998 by the World Kindness Movement, a coalition of kindness organisations from several countries. It encourages small acts of kindness towards others. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday.',
    "world-childrens-day": "World Children's Day on 20 November is the United Nations day for children. The date marks the adoption of the Declaration of the Rights of the Child in 1959 and of the Convention on the Rights of the Child in 1989. It was first established in 1954 as Universal Children's Day, and UNICEF now runs it with events led by children.",
    "world-aids-day": 'World AIDS Day on 1 December was first held in 1988, on the initiative of the World Health Organization. People wear red ribbons to show support for people living with HIV and to remember those who have died of AIDS-related illnesses. It is an awareness day, not a public holiday.',
    "human-rights-day": "Human Rights Day on 10 December marks the adoption of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights by the United Nations General Assembly in 1948. The day has been observed since 1950. The Nobel Peace Prize is presented in Oslo on the same date, the anniversary of Alfred Nobel's death.",
    "thanksgiving-us": "Thanksgiving in the United States falls on the fourth Thursday of November and is a federal holiday, traditionally marked with a family meal. Canada celebrates its own Thanksgiving on the second Monday of October.",
    "black-friday": "Black Friday is the day after US Thanksgiving and the traditional start of the Christmas shopping season, with discounts in shops and online. The idea has spread well beyond the United States, but it is not a public holiday.",
    "cyber-monday": "Cyber Monday is the Monday after US Thanksgiving and is aimed at online shopping deals. The term was introduced in 2005 by the US National Retail Federation. It is a shopping event, not a public holiday.",
    "summer-solstice": "The June solstice happens around 20 or 21 June. It is the longest day of the year in the northern hemisphere and the shortest in the southern hemisphere. The exact moment changes from year to year, so this page counts down to 21 June as a close approximation.",
    "winter-solstice": "The December solstice happens around 21 or 22 December. It is the shortest day in the northern hemisphere and the longest in the southern hemisphere. The exact moment changes from year to year, so this page counts down to 21 December as a close approximation.",
}

